#{[{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046645} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532757} :activity/id #uuid "15816cbf-9c2c-53ef-a829-ca2321defe44" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049265 :topic/id #uuid "e80c9bca-b203-5866-835e-d0d29bbfdf22" :topic/math-academy-id 1995} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532739 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129490 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0fd56eb4-4c51-5eff-a136-8a8cbe4d2081" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{32\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531963} {:answer/id #uuid "26b58db8-aa9b-5306-acc7-0ae354e19ac8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{48\\pi }{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532013} {:answer/id #uuid "d30855cf-2561-551f-9f08-0885a43147ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{63\\pi }{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532385} {:answer/id #uuid "e76641f1-4b1e-5f93-a7e4-384fd90837d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{24\\pi }{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532432} {:answer/id #uuid "e78f34b4-d5a7-5009-8033-283511cf7d05" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{56\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532433}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532013} :answer-field/id #uuid "fded47ac-9666-5e9a-a118-02ceca041d7e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126440}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "294bf3af-6f1c-5f27-8904-a3e6a8a75f11" :question/math-academy-id "q-138970" :question/problem "Consider the curve defined parametrically as \n\n$$\nx=3t-{t}^{3},\\,y=3{t}^{2},\\,t\\in [0,1].\n$$\n\nCalculate the surface area generated when this curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis." :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi y\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince our curve is given in parametric form, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}y\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from ${t}_{1}=0$ to ${t}_{2}=1.$ \n\nComputing the derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3t-{t}^{3})=3-3{t}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3{t}^{2})=6t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}y\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}3{t}^{2}\\sqrt{{(3-3{t}^{2})}^{2}+{(6t)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}3{t}^{2}\\sqrt{9-18{t}^{2}+9{t}^{4}+36{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}3{t}^{2}\\sqrt{9{t}^{4}+18{t}^{2}+9}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, factoring the expression $9{t}^{4}+18{t}^{2}+9,$ we can rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}3{t}^{2}\\sqrt{9{t}^{4}+18{t}^{2}+9}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}3{t}^{2}\\sqrt{9({t}^{4}+2{t}^{2}+1)}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}3{t}^{2}\\cdot 3\\sqrt{{t}^{4}+2{t}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =18\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}{t}^{2}\\sqrt{({t}^{2}+1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =18\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}{t}^{2}\\cdot |{t}^{2}+1|\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =18\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}{t}^{2}({t}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =18\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}({t}^{4}+{t}^{2})\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =18\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}({t}^{4}+{t}^{2})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =18\\pi \\cdot {[\\frac{{t}^{5}}{5}+\\frac{{t}^{3}}{3}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =18\\pi [(\\frac{1}{5}+\\frac{1}{3})-(\\frac{0}{5}+\\frac{0}{3})] \\\\  & =18\\pi \\cdot \\frac{8}{15} \\\\  & =\\frac{48\\pi }{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "57085c22-32d0-5a83-a69e-4ce3a9bdecbd" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532742}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532742 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532700 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4d11b877-fb53-589c-8eea-da397cb6e4c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "27\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532095} {:answer/id #uuid "9873e93d-4b80-59cd-bde4-4c4887e98e61" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{9}\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532281} {:answer/id #uuid "dc7fb2c2-362a-55ce-b957-f29bf7d97ca4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "27\\sqrt{3}\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532407} {:answer/id #uuid "e033b1bb-f523-513b-b42b-df8a9a20816a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "9\\sqrt{3}\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532412} {:answer/id #uuid "fd4836d1-dcb7-5a07-b0af-0193ffb18029" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "9\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532488}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532095} :answer-field/id #uuid "9b1becb6-0d80-5be3-8b07-052ebc9be8d2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532564}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "bec28ae8-3800-5ebe-aea9-25248c908bf5" :question/math-academy-id "q-139031" :question/problem "Consider the curve defined parametrically as \n\n$$\nx=3{t}^{2},\\,y={t}^{3}-3t,\\,t\\in [-\\sqrt{3},0].\n$$\n\nCalculate the surface area generated when this curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis." :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi y\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince our curve is given in parametric form, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}y\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from ${t}_{1}=-\\sqrt{3}$ to ${t}_{2}=0.$ \n\nComputing the derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3{t}^{2})=6t \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3}-3t)=3{t}^{2}-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}y\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{-\\sqrt{3}}^{0}({t}^{3}-3t)\\sqrt{{(6t)}^{2}+{(3{t}^{2}-3)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{-\\sqrt{3}}^{0}({t}^{3}-3t)\\sqrt{36{t}^{2}+9{t}^{4}-18{t}^{2}+9}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{-\\sqrt{3}}^{0}({t}^{3}-3t)\\sqrt{9{t}^{4}+18{t}^{2}+9}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, factoring the expression $9{t}^{4}+18{t}^{2}+9,$ we can rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{-\\sqrt{3}}^{0}({t}^{3}-3t)\\sqrt{9{t}^{4}+18{t}^{2}+9}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{-\\sqrt{3}}^{0}({t}^{3}-3t)\\sqrt{9({t}^{4}+2{t}^{2}+1)}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{-\\sqrt{3}}^{0}({t}^{3}-3t)\\cdot 3\\sqrt{{t}^{4}+2{t}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =6\\pi {\\int }_{-\\sqrt{3}}^{0}({t}^{3}-3t)\\sqrt{({t}^{2}+1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =6\\pi {\\int }_{-\\sqrt{3}}^{0}({t}^{3}-3t)|{t}^{2}+1|\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =6\\pi {\\int }_{-\\sqrt{3}}^{0}({t}^{3}-3t)({t}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =6\\pi {\\int }_{-\\sqrt{3}}^{0}({t}^{5}-2{t}^{3}-3t)\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =6\\pi {\\int }_{-\\sqrt{3}}^{0}({t}^{5}-2{t}^{3}-3t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =6\\pi \\cdot {[\\frac{{t}^{6}}{6}-\\frac{{t}^{4}}{2}-\\frac{3{t}^{2}}{2}]}_{-\\sqrt{3}}^{0} \\\\  & =6\\pi [0-(\\frac{27}{6}-\\frac{9}{2}-\\frac{9}{2})] \\\\  & =6\\pi \\cdot \\frac{27}{6} \\\\  & =27\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "64958667-b549-5eb7-8561-a3974fe10b64" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532743}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532743 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532658 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e381238e-91d4-5561-a74f-947fada01bf0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532419}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532419} :answer-field/id #uuid "1a1dc858-f317-5a50-ac0a-63bd26d09cc3" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532505}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "568d7106-115a-5160-be7c-9ea466db4771" :question/math-academy-id "q-352091" :question/problem "Consider the curve defined parametrically as \n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t),\\,y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{sec}⁡(t)+\\operatorname{tan}⁡(t)|-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t),\\,t\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{3}].\n$$\n\nDetermine the surface area $S$ generated when this curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis. Do not round the answer.\n\n$S=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi x\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince our curve is given in parametric form, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}x\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from ${t}_{1}=0$ to ${t}_{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$ \n\nComputing the derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)]=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t), \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{sec}⁡(t)+\\operatorname{tan}⁡(t)|-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)]=\\operatorname{sec}⁡(t)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\sqrt{{[-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)]}^{2}+{[\\operatorname{sec}⁡(t)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)]}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡(t)+{sec}^{2}⁡(t)-2+{cos}^{2}⁡(t)}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\sqrt{{sec}^{2}⁡(t)-2+[{sin}^{2}⁡(t)+{cos}^{2}⁡(t)]}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the identity ${sin}^{2}⁡(t)+{cos}^{2}⁡(t)=1$ and ${sec}^{2}⁡(t)-1={tan}^{2}⁡(t)$ we can rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\sqrt{{sec}^{2}⁡(t)-2+[{sin}^{2}⁡(t)+{cos}^{2}⁡(t)]}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\sqrt{{sec}^{2}⁡(t)-2+1}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\sqrt{{sec}^{2}⁡(t)-1}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\sqrt{{tan}^{2}⁡(t)}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\cdot |\\operatorname{tan}⁡(t)|\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\cdot \\operatorname{tan}⁡(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi [-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)]_{0}^{\\pi /3} \\\\  & =-2\\pi [\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)] \\\\  & =-2\\pi (\\frac{1}{2}-1) \\\\  & =-2\\pi \\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "679ce636-9dcd-5b15-ab39-bb3b7ed24f1c"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532747 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532703 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2dd285d1-cf9d-5646-a446-ce41bd2e2458" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{52\\pi }{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532024} {:answer/id #uuid "c01060b2-f26a-567b-a1dd-ba792972b5d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{32\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532346} {:answer/id #uuid "ef41bf2a-c7d0-5536-922b-f7eb8c9ec0ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{40\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532450} {:answer/id #uuid "f173cc96-34cf-5cea-9d45-09f0f19aa1e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{48\\pi }{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532455} {:answer/id #uuid "f9f80523-10c6-5bea-b36a-01b7f0de1f7e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{21\\pi }{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532477}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532024} :answer-field/id #uuid "d460285e-96c9-5656-88a1-24adf10022f6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532593}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "c339d0f2-af34-5938-bbf8-d47914ddf165" :question/math-academy-id "q-138967" :question/problem "Consider the curve defined parametrically as \n\n$$\nx={t}^{2}-1,\\,y=\\frac{{t}^{3}}{3}-t,\\,t\\in [1,2].\n$$\n\nCalculate the surface area generated when this curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis." :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi x\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince our curve is given in parametric form, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}x\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from ${t}_{1}=1$ to ${t}_{2}=2.$ \n\nComputing the derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}-1)=2t \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{{t}^{3}}{3}-t)={t}^{2}-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}x\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{2}({t}^{2}-1)\\sqrt{{(2t)}^{2}+{({t}^{2}-1)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{2}({t}^{2}-1)\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+{t}^{4}-2{t}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{2}({t}^{2}-1)\\sqrt{{t}^{4}+2{t}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, factoring the expression ${t}^{4}+2{t}^{2}+1,$ we can rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{2}({t}^{2}-1)\\sqrt{{t}^{4}+2{t}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{2}({t}^{2}-1)\\sqrt{{({t}^{2})}^{2}+2\\cdot 1\\cdot {t}^{2}+{1}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{2}({t}^{2}-1)\\sqrt{{({t}^{2}+1)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{2}({t}^{2}-1)\\cdot |{t}^{2}+1|\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{2}({t}^{2}-1)\\cdot ({t}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{2}({t}^{4}-1)\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using direct integration we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{2}({t}^{4}-1)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi \\cdot {[\\frac{{t}^{5}}{5}-t]}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =2\\pi [(\\frac{{2}^{5}}{5}-2)-(\\frac{{1}^{5}}{5}-1)] \\\\  & =2\\pi [\\frac{22}{5}+\\frac{4}{5}] \\\\  & =2\\pi \\cdot \\frac{26}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{52\\pi }{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "a37727ba-408c-54ca-b44b-c58bf3c5ee21" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532739}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532757 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532670 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "397b7274-6f31-5ec6-8f5f-76084e482538" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}2{t}^{3}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532049} {:answer/id #uuid "463add96-423b-5d1f-a988-936fb0b73d81" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}2t(4t+1)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532075} {:answer/id #uuid "6cc93547-60e1-5394-aaaf-3f2640834b2d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}({t}^{2}+1)\\sqrt{2t+1}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532179} {:answer/id #uuid "8aa4f95f-378d-5338-8b6f-85c27742f8b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}(t+1)\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532249} {:answer/id #uuid "8f9b8633-af16-5b49-b623-797c047243a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}{t}^{2}\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532259}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532249} :answer-field/id #uuid "18ee6b17-46b2-5260-a155-51e284324ae6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532504}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "7d6c3d2a-2d0d-538a-8df1-4fa8a05510f8" :question/math-academy-id "q-138965" :question/problem "Consider the curve defined parametrically as \n\n$$\nx={t}^{2},\\,y=t+1,\\,t\\in [0,2].\n$$\n\nWhat integral gives the surface area generated when this curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis?" :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi y\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince our curve is given in parametric form, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}y\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from ${t}_{1}=0$ to ${t}_{2}=2.$ \n\nComputing the derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2})=2t \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t+1)=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}y\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}(t+1)\\sqrt{{(2t)}^{2}+{(1)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}(t+1)\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "d839e236-0c85-509d-9ef3-41872a77d0ff" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532747}}] :activity/title "Surface Areas of Revolution for Parametric Curves" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531930}]}
